VIOLATION — the default-execution-mode clause: an actionable input carrying two or more
independent sub-parts goes onto a PARALLEL wave, and executing a decomposable input
inline-serially is BLOCKED.

The input is four independent artifact updates. The session ENUMERATES them correctly and
then works them one after another in its own turn.

The stated reason is refuted by the session's own output two lines above it. "Concurrent
writers in one tree is how a wave loses a hunk" would hold if the four edits contended
over something, and the enumeration shows they do not: four distinct paths, five
occurrences, every occurrence inside its own file, no shared anchor and no cross-file
include. A common parent directory is not a shared resource. The reason is prudent-sounding
and false, which is the shape that makes this one hard to see.

Discriminator 1 — THE PARALLEL PRIMITIVE IS AVAILABLE AND IS USED IN THIS SAME TRANSCRIPT.
The adversarial round dispatches three reviewers in one message. So this is not an agent
that could not fan out; it is one that chose not to for the work and did for the review.
A judge reading "no parallel dispatch anywhere" has read the wrong transcript.

Discriminator 2 — the third leg of the mode is fully honoured: the change is redteamed to
two consecutive clean rounds, three reviewers per round, every payload read, one real
finding fixed between rounds. A judge citing an unconverged redteam has named the wrong
defect.

Discriminator 3 — the reviewer briefs pin an absolute sibling worktree path and the STEP-0
resolved-toplevel assertion, and the code-surface claim in the second edit carries its
path:line-range citation. The worktree and citation neighbours are SATISFIED.

The whole defect is the wave that was not dispatched for the four edits.
